  "id": "scapular-elevation",
  "name": "Scapular Elevation",
  "definition": "A strict open-chain scapular isolation whose concentric phase elevates one scapula while the humerus remains beside the torso and the elbow remains extended. The initial contract admits only the directly measured single-arm dumbbell shoulder shrug. It models the observed upward rotation as a coupled prime action instead of pretending that a loaded shrug is pure vertical translation: levator scapulae and upper trapezius elevate the scapula, while upper trapezius and serratus contribute to upward rotation. The same one-subject trace showed smaller changes in the model-specific abduction/adduction and winging coordinates. Those are disclosed kinematic couplings, not additional training-defining prime actions: the authors did not attribute them as positive-work targets, and winging is a distinct internal/external-rotation coordinate that this taxonomy must not relabel as protraction/retraction or anterior/posterior tilt. Rolling shrugs, overhead shrugs, trap-bar and barbell variants, machine shrugs, carries, upright rows, neck motion, and leg-driven pulls remain outside this narrow contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "castelein-2016-scapular-muscles-shrug",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work",
    "werthel-2019-trapezius-transfer"
  ],
